x86_64-linux  cxx1 writes the C1 and D1 forms of the implicit copy and move constructors of `Base`, `Both`, `Derived` and `Pair`, and of the constructors `Mv`, `Base`, `Both`, `Poly`, `Derived`, `Pair` and `Boom` define inside their classes, where clang emits only the C2 and D2 a use asks for (docs/CONFORMANCE.md, "An inline member function is a strong symbol"). The compiler-written copies and moves have no out-of-line form, and running them through the Builder is the point of the case.
arm64-darwin  the same rule for the implicit copies clang folds into their callers, and for `__cxx1_vec_new_`/`_del_`, the array loops cxx1 names and clang writes inline at each `new[]`, `delete[]` and static array.
x86_64-windows  cl and clang spell the array loops inline (`??_L`/`??_M` as cl's vector constructor and destructor iterators), where cxx1 emits `__cxx1_vec_new_`/`_del_` functions of its own; the exit helper is cxx1's `atexit` registration against clang's own.
